oke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="17" y="86" font-size="12" text-anchor="end" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">3</text><line x1="20" y1="34" x2="24" y2="34" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="17" y="34" font-size="12" text-anchor="end" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">4</text><text x="203" y="274" font-size="14" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">V / m³</text><text x="20" y="14" font-size="14" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">p / 10⁵ Pa</text><polygon points="232.8,86 322.3,86 322.3,242 232.8,242" style="stroke:none;stroke-width:0.9;fill:var(--fig-fill,rgba(29,78,216,0.10));stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polyline points="322.3,86 232.8,86" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/></svg>', 'p–V graph: a horizontal line at 3.0 × 10⁵ Pa from 0.050 m³ to 0.035 m³; the shaded area is the work done on the gas.'),
('44ffd8ac-34e1-5888-9e72-777bb1795a13', '732e83dd-2c22-497f-8d64-2f7a3fd69629', 'ac0eb546-c37c-5122-8eaa-89bbcb42a5c1', 'a-level', '19.2', '9702 coursebook 19.2 · The first law of thermodynamics', 28, 28, 'A gas undergoes an isothermal expansion. Which statement is correct?', NULL, 2, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'The heat supplied equals the work done by the gas', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Isothermal: ΔU = 0, so q = −w: all the heat supplied is used to do work. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: ΔU = 0. C1: q = −w. A1: C (The heat supplied equals the work done by the gas).', 3, NULL, NULL),
('8f3168f9-57d2-5cec-8eb5-38733c3cc502', '732e83dd-2c22-497f-8d64-2f7a3fd69629', 'ac0eb546-c37c-5122-8eaa-89bbcb42a5c1', 'a-level', '19.2', '9702 coursebook 19.2 · The first law of thermodynamics', 29, 29, 'In a diesel engine, air is compressed so fast that it is effectively adiabatic. 400 J of work is done on the air, which has 0.020 mol. Calculate the rise in internal energy, and explain why the air becomes hot. Give the rise.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '400', 'J', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'q = 0 (adiabatic), so ΔU = q + w = 0 + 400 = 400 J. The molecules'' mean kinetic energy increases, so the temperature rises enough to ignite the fuel. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: q = 0. C1: ΔU = w. B1: temperature rises.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('cbf73448-ec8b-51e2-9af6-8b284f83d357', '732e83dd-2c22-497f-8d64-2f7a3fd69629', 'ac0eb546-c37c-5122-8eaa-89bbcb42a5c1', 'a-level', '19.2', '9702 coursebook 19.2 · The first law of thermodynamics', 30, 30, 'An ideal gas is heated at constant pressure. Compared with heating the same gas by the same temperature at constant volume, which is correct?', NULL, 2, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'More heat is needed at constant pressure, because the gas also does work as it expands', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'ΔU is the same for the same temperature rise. At constant pressure w = −pΔV < 0, so q = ΔU − w is larger. Mark scheme (4 marks). C1: ΔU depends only on T. C1: work done at constant p. C1: q larger. A1: A (More heat is needed at constant pressure, because the gas also does work as it expands).', 4, NULL, NULL),
('1dbab559-115a-571a-b2de-5ff871722abe', '732e83dd-2c22-497f-8d64-2f7a3fd69629', 'ac0eb546-c37c-5122-8eaa-89bbcb42a5c1', 'a-level', '19.2', '9702 coursebook 19.2 · The first law of thermodynamics', 31, 31, 'A gas cylinder''s piston (area 0.015 m²) is pushed in 8.0 cm against a constant gas pressure of 2.4 × 10⁵ Pa, while 120 J escapes by heating. Calculate ΔU.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '168', 'J', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'w = pAd = 2.4 × 10⁵ × 0.015 × 0.080 = 288 J. ΔU = q + w = −120 + 288 = 168 J. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: ΔV = Ad. C1: w = pΔV positive. C1: ΔU.', 3, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 320 300" role="img" aria-labelledby="fg6610c4bb-t fg6610c4bb-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fg6610c4bb-t">Compressing gas with a piston</title><desc id="fg6610c4bb-d">A piston of area 0.015 m² is pushed 8.0 cm into a cylinder against a constant gas pressure of 2.4 × 10⁵ Pa.</desc><line x1="100" y1="60" x2="100" y2="270" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.6;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="220" y1="60" x2="220" y2="270" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.6;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="100" y1="270" x2="220" y2="270" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.6;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><rect x="102" y="142" width="116" height="126" rx="0" style="stroke:none;stroke-width:0.9;fill:var(--fig-fill,rgba(29,78,216,0.10));stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><rect x="102" y="130" width="116" height="12" rx="0" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="160" y="206" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">gas</text><text x="160" y="288" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">piston area 0.015 m²</text><text x="160" y="40" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">gas at 2.4 × 10⁵ Pa; piston pushed in 8.0 cm</text></svg>', 'A piston of area 0.015 m² is pushed 8.0 cm into a cylinder against a constant gas pressure of 2.4 × 10⁵ Pa.'),
('94fcc5af-b622-52e3-a4eb-57635e24fb11', '732e83dd-2c22-497f-8d64-2f7a3fd69629', 'ac0eb546-c37c-5122-8eaa-89bbcb42a5c1', 'a-level', '19.2', '9702 coursebook 19.2 · The first law of thermodynamics', 32, 32, 'A p–V graph shows a gas expanding at constant pressure 4.0 × 10⁵ Pa from 1.0 × 10⁻³ m³ to 3.5 × 10⁻³ m³. Calculate the area under the line and state what it represents. Give the area.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '1000', 'J', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Area = pΔV = 4.0 × 10⁵ × 2.5 × 10⁻³ = 1000 J: the work done by the gas. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: area = pΔV. B1: work done by gas. A1: W = 1000 J.', 3, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 400 290" role="img" aria-labelledby="fgeed6b7af-t fgeed6b7af-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fgeed6b7af-t">p–V graph</title><desc id="fgeed6b7af-d">p–V graph: the gas expands at a constant 4.0 × 10⁵ Pa from 1.0 × 10⁻³ m³ to 3.5 × 10⁻³ m³; the area under the line is shaded.</desc><line x1="24" y1="34" x2="24" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="109.2" y1="34" x2="109.2" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="194.5" y1="34" x2="194.5" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="279.7" y1="34" x2="279.7" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="365" y1="34" x2="365" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="24" y1="242" x2="382" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="24" y1="200.4" x2="382" y2="200.4" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="24" y1="158.8" x2="382" y2="158.8" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="24" y1="117.2" x2="382" y2="117.2" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="24" y1="75.6" x2="382" y2="75.6" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="24" y1="34" x2="382" y2="34" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="24" y1="242" x2="388" y2="242" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:1.4;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="24" y1="242" x2="24" y2="28" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:1.4;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="392,242 385.7,245 385.7,239" style="fill:currentColor;stroke:none"/><polygon points="24,24 27,30.3 21,30.3" style="fill:currentColor;stroke:none"/><line x1="24" y1="242" x2="24" y2="246" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="24" y="254" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">0</text><line x1="109.2" y1="242" x2="109.2" y2="246" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="109.2" y="254" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">1</text><line x1="194.5" y1="242" x2="194.5" y2="246" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="194.5" y="254" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">2</text><line x1="279.7" y1="242" x2="279.7" y2="246" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="279.7" y="254" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">3</text><line x1="365" y1="242" x2="365" y2="246" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="365" y="254" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">4</text><line x1="20" y1="242" x2="24" y2="242" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="17" y="242" font-size="12" text-anchor="end" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">0</text><line x1="20" y1="200.4" x2="24" y2="200.4" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="17" y="200.4" font-size="12" text-anchor="end" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">1</text><line x1="20" y1="158.8" x2="24" y2="158.8" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="17" y="158.8" font-size="12" text-anchor="end" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">2</text><line x1="20" y1="117.2" x2="24" y2="117.2" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="17" y="117.2" font-size="12" text-anchor="end" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">3</text><line x1="20" y1="75.6" x2="24" y2="75.6" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="17" y="75.6" font-size="12" text-anchor="end" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">4</text><line x1="20" y1="34" x2="24" y2="34" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="17" y="34" font-size="12" text-anchor="end" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">5</text><text x="203" y="274" font-size="14" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">V / 10⁻³ m³</text><text x="20" y="14" font-size="14" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">p / 10⁵ Pa</text><polygon points="109.2,75.6 322.3,75.6 322.3,242 109.2,242" style="stroke:none;stroke-width:0.9;fill:var(--fig-fill,rgba(29,78,216,0.10));stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polyline points="109.2,75.6 322.3,75.6" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/></svg>', 'p–V graph: the gas expands at a constant 4.0 × 10⁵ Pa from 1.0 × 10⁻³ m³ to 3.5 × 10⁻³ m³; the area under the line is shaded.'),
('a15f6dc4-ebf8-5b31-bf54-e22493eadab0', '732e83dd-2c22-497f-8d64-2f7a3fd69629', 'ac0eb546-c37c-5122-8eaa-89bbcb42a5c1', 'a-level', '19.2', '9702 coursebook 19.2 · The first law of thermodynamics', 33, 33, 'A sealed rigid container of gas is heated. What happens to the work done and the internal energy?', NULL, 2, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'No work is done and all the heat increases the internal energy', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Rigid means ΔV = 0, so w = 0 and ΔU = q. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: ΔV = 0 so w = 0. C1: ΔU = q. A1: D (No work is done and all the heat increases the internal energy).', 3, NULL, NULL),
('c66fc181-68ee-5cd7-891c-92cf39e991c9', '732e83dd-2c22-497f-8d64-2f7a3fd69629', 'ac0eb546-c37c-5122-8eaa-89bbcb42a5c1', 'a-level', '19.2', '9702 coursebook 19.2 · The first law of thermodynamics', 34, 34, 'An ideal gas expands isothermally, doing 650 J of work. It is then compressed adiabatically back to its original volume with 820 J of work done on it. Calculate the total change in internal energy over both stages.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '820', 'J', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Stage 1 (isothermal): ΔU₁ = 0. Stage 2 (adiabatic): q = 0, ΔU₂ = w = +820 J. Total ΔU = 0 + 820 = 820 J. Mark scheme (4 marks). C1: isothermal ΔU = 0. C1: adiabatic ΔU = w. C1: total. A1: ΔU = 820 J.', 4, NULL, NULL),
('4fe45449-968f-5b48-a598-c39e57c0264d', '732e83dd-2c22-497f-8d64-2f7a3fd69629', 'ac0eb546-c37c-5122-8eaa-89bbcb42a5c1', 'a-level', '19.2', '9702 coursebook 19.2 · The first law of thermodynamics', 35, 35, 'A gas cools at constant volume. Which describes q, w and ΔU?', NULL, 2, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'q < 0, w = 0, ΔU < 0', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Constant volume: w = 0. Cooling: energy leaves, q < 0, so ΔU = q < 0. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: w = 0. C1: q and ΔU negative. A1: B (q < 0, w = 0, ΔU < 0).', 3, NULL, NULL),
('22b8fb01-2ed0-52ad-a80e-f6f9d6c7097b', '732e83dd-2c22-497f-8d64-2f7a3fd69629', 'ac0eb546-c37c-5122-8eaa-89bbcb42a5c1', 'a-level', '19.2', '9702 coursebook 19.2 · The first law of thermodynamics', 36, 36, 'A gas is heated with 2.0 kJ; its internal energy increases by 1.4 kJ. Calculate the work done by the gas, and state whether it expanded or was compressed. Give the work done by the gas.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '600', 'J', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'w = ΔU − q = 1400 − 2000 = −600 J. The work done BY the gas = q − ΔU = 2000 − 1400 = 600 J: it expanded. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: w = ΔU − q. B1: sign interpretation. A1: W = 600 J.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('3da1959c-c7df-5e84-aefb-61b60b038e8b', '732e83dd-2c22-497f-8d64-2f7a3fd69629', 'ac0eb546-c37c-5122-8eaa-89bbcb42a5c1', 'a-level', '19.2', '9702 coursebook 19.2 · The first law of thermodynamics', 37, 37, 'A cylinder contains gas at 1.0 × 10⁵ Pa. 500 J of heat is supplied and the gas expands at constant pressure, increasing its internal energy by 300 J. Calculate the increase in volume.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '0.002', 'm³', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'w = ΔU − q = 300 − 500 = −200 J, so the gas did 200 J of work. ΔV = W/p = 200 ÷ 1.0 × 10⁵ = 2.0 × 10⁻³ m³. Mark scheme (4 marks). C1: w = ΔU − q. C1: W = pΔV. C1: rearranged. A1: ΔV = 0.0020 m³.', 4, NULL, NULL),
('f23bb73d-a3ae-5180-bda2-a6af7d627a0d', '732e83dd-2c22-497f-8d64-2f7a3fd69629', 'ac0eb546-c37c-5122-8eaa-89bbcb42a5c1', 'a-level', '19.2', '9702 coursebook 19.2 · The first law of thermodynamics', 38, 38, 'Why do the gas and nozzle cool when an aerosol can is sprayed?', NULL, 2, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'The gas expands quickly, doing work on the surroundings with little heat entering, so its internal energy falls', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Rapid expansion ≈ adiabatic: q ≈ 0 and w < 0, so ΔU < 0 and the temperature falls. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: rapid expansion, q ≈ 0. C1: w negative so ΔU negative. A1: C (The gas expands quickly, doing work on the surroundings with little heat entering, so its internal energy falls).', 3, NULL, NULL),
('92a9aea1-7c93-5300-9cc6-55c5d9432b70', '732e83dd-2c22-497f-8d64-2f7a3fd69629', 'ac0eb546-c37c-5122-8eaa-89bbcb42a5c1', 'a-level', '19.2', '9702 coursebook 19.2 · The first law of thermodynamics', 39, 39, 'A gas is compressed adiabatically and its internal energy rises by 240 J. It then cools at constant volume, losing 90 J. Calculate the final change in internal energy from the start.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '150', 'J', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Stage 1: ΔU = +240 J. Stage 2: ΔU = q = −90 J. Total = 240 − 90 = 150 J. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: stage 1. C1: stage 2 w = 0. A1: ΔU = 150 J.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('2b7e963b-52a3-51fd-9a4c-d9124bf438a3', '732e83dd-2c22-497f-8d64-2f7a3fd69629', 'ac0eb546-c37c-5122-8eaa-89bbcb42a5c1', 'a-level', '19.2', '9702 coursebook 19.2 · The first law of thermodynamics', 40, 40, 'A gas in a syringe is pushed in: 25 J of work is done on it while it loses 10 J by heating. Calculate ΔU.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '15', 'J', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'ΔU = q + w = −10 + 25 = 15 J. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: q negative. C1: w positive. A1: ΔU = 15 J.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('192b1070-ebea-57de-8399-3bd4cf48c846', '732e83dd-2c22-497f-8d64-2f7a3fd69629', 'ac0eb546-c37c-5122-8eaa-89bbcb42a5c1', 'a-level', '19.2', '9702 coursebook 19.2 · The first law of thermodynamics', 41, 41, 'A gas does 1.5 kJ of work as it expands from 2.0 × 10⁻³ m³ at a constant pressure. Its volume becomes 9.5 × 10⁻³ m³. Calculate the pressure.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '2e5', 'Pa', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'ΔV = 9.5 × 10⁻³ − 2.0 × 10⁻³ = 7.5 × 10⁻³ m³. W = pΔV, so p = W/ΔV = 1500 ÷ 7.5 × 10⁻³ = 2.0 × 10⁵ Pa. Mark scheme (4 marks). C1: ΔV. C1: W = pΔV. C1: rearranged. A1: p = 2.0 × 10⁵ Pa.', 4, NULL, NULL),
('aacb9a15-5670-5d9d-8ed5-09338574c6c5', '732e83dd-2c22-497f-8d64-2f7a3fd69629', 'ac0eb546-c37c-5122-8eaa-89bbcb42a5c1', 'a-level', '19.2', '9702 coursebook 19.2 · The first law of thermodynamics', 42, 42, 'A gas is taken round a cycle ABCA. A → B: heated at constant volume, 500 J supplied. B → C: expands isothermally, doing 350 J of work. C → A: compressed at constant pressure 1.0 × 10⁵ Pa by 2.0 × 10⁻³ m³. (a) Calculate w for C → A. (b) Use ΔU = 0 for the cycle to find the net heat exchanged. (c) Calculate the heat transferred in C → A, stating its direction. Give only the answer to (c).', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '-700', 'J', false, NULL, true, NULL, '(a) w = +pΔV = 1.0 × 10⁵ × 2.0 × 10⁻³ = +200 J (work done on the gas). (b) Net w = 0 − 350 + 200 = −150 J, so net q = +150 J. (c) ΔU: A→B +500 J; B→C 0; C→A must be −500 J to close the cycle, so q = ΔU − w = −500 − 200 = −700 J: 700 J leaves the gas. Mark scheme (6 marks). A1: w = +200 J. C1: ΔU = 0 for cycle. A1: net q. C1: ΔU for C → A. C1: q = ΔU − w. A1: q = −700 J.', 6, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 400 290" role="img" aria-labelledby="fg2c396d37-t fg2c396d37-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fg2c396d37-t">Cycle ABCA</title><desc id="fg2c396d37-d">p–V diagram of cycle ABCA: A to B is a vertical line (constant volume, 500 J supplied), B to C is an isothermal expansion curve (350 J of work done by the gas), and C to A is a horizontal compression at 1.0 × 10⁵ Pa by 2.0 × 10⁻³ m³.</desc><line x1="86.9" y1="242" x2="388" y2="242" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:1.4;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="86.9" y1="242" x2="86.9" y2="28" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:1.4;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="392,242 385.7,245 385.7,239" style="fill:currentColor;stroke:none"/><polygon points="86.9,24 