('2db94dfc-b424-55b6-a34f-e7d399428fd0', '732e83dd-2c22-497f-8d64-2f7a3fd69629', 'ea4a2b5f-fc7b-5e52-84b7-b92896426bdd', 'a-level', '19.6', '9702 coursebook 19.6 · Specific heat capacity and latent heat', 9, 9, 'A swimming pool holds 3.0 × 10⁵ kg of water. Calculate the energy needed to warm it by 2.0 K. Take the specific heat capacity of water as 4200 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹.', NULL, 1, 'numeric'::answer_type, '2.52e9', 'J', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'E = mcΔθ = 3.0 × 10⁵ × 4200 × 2.0 = 2.52 × 10⁹ J. Mark scheme (2 marks). C1: E = mcΔθ. A1: E = 2.52 × 10⁹ J.', 2, NULL, NULL),
('c93e9ef1-32b8-54bd-a10c-3f188db778bc', '732e83dd-2c22-497f-8d64-2f7a3fd69629', 'ea4a2b5f-fc7b-5e52-84b7-b92896426bdd', 'a-level', '19.6', '9702 coursebook 19.6 · Specific heat capacity and latent heat', 10, 10, 'A 0.50 kg steel bolt (c = 450 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹) absorbs 9000 J. Calculate its temperature rise.', NULL, 1, 'numeric'::answer_type, '40', 'K', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Δθ = E/(mc) = 9000 ÷ (0.50 × 450) = 40 K. Mark scheme (2 marks). C1: Δθ = E/mc. A1: Δθ = 40 K.', 2, NULL, NULL),
('69344d8e-4ebb-5aa6-a5d9-2418ace337be', '732e83dd-2c22-497f-8d64-2f7a3fd69629', 'ea4a2b5f-fc7b-5e52-84b7-b92896426bdd', 'a-level', '19.6', '9702 coursebook 19.6 · Specific heat capacity and latent heat', 11, 11, 'A data book lists a value of 900 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹ for aluminium. What does this specific heat capacity tell you?', NULL, 1, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, '900 J is needed to raise the temperature of 1 kg of aluminium by 1 K', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Specific heat capacity c = E/(mΔθ) is the energy per kilogram per kelvin of temperature rise. Mark scheme (1 mark). B1: C (900 J is needed to raise the temperature of 1 kg of aluminium by 1 K).', 1, NULL, NULL),
('99a30584-e7fe-5c18-b164-44e03293fb78', '732e83dd-2c22-497f-8d64-2f7a3fd69629', 'ea4a2b5f-fc7b-5e52-84b7-b92896426bdd', 'a-level', '19.6', '9702 coursebook 19.6 · Specific heat capacity and latent heat', 12, 12, 'What is the SI unit of specific heat capacity?', NULL, 1, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'c = E/(mΔθ): joules per kilogram per kelvin. Mark scheme (1 mark). B1: A (J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹).', 1, NULL, NULL),
('0d66496a-dff5-5782-b183-f50fe67cfe36', '732e83dd-2c22-497f-8d64-2f7a3fd69629', 'ea4a2b5f-fc7b-5e52-84b7-b92896426bdd', 'a-level', '19.6', '9702 coursebook 19.6 · Specific heat capacity and latent heat', 13, 13, 'A 1.5 kg block absorbs 54 000 J and warms by 40 K. Calculate the specific heat capacity of its material.', NULL, 1, 'numeric'::answer_type, '900', 'J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'c = E/(mΔθ) = 54000 ÷ (1.5 × 40) = 900 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹. Mark scheme (2 marks). C1: c = E/mΔθ. A1: c = 900 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹.', 2, NULL, NULL),
('bf94863f-67b4-58d8-afaf-58c51194711b', '732e83dd-2c22-497f-8d64-2f7a3fd69629', 'ea4a2b5f-fc7b-5e52-84b7-b92896426bdd', 'a-level', '19.6', '9702 coursebook 19.6 · Specific heat capacity and latent heat', 14, 14, 'Why is water used as the coolant in car engines?', NULL, 1, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'Its high specific heat capacity lets it absorb a lot of energy for a small temperature rise', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'c ≈ 4200 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹ is among the highest of common liquids. Mark scheme (1 mark). B1: D (Its high specific heat capacity lets it absorb a lot of energy for a small temperature rise).', 1, NULL, NULL),
('6763ccbb-1a04-59bb-98d5-f5d02fe2a8c6', '732e83dd-2c22-497f-8d64-2f7a3fd69629', 'ea4a2b5f-fc7b-5e52-84b7-b92896426bdd', 'a-level', '19.6', '9702 coursebook 19.6 · Specific heat capacity and latent heat', 15, 15, 'Calculate the energy needed to heat 0.75 kg of milk (c = 3900 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹) from 5 °C to 65 °C.', NULL, 1, 'numeric'::answer_type, '1.755e5', 'J', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'E = mcΔθ = 0.75 × 3900 × 60 = 175500 J. Mark scheme (2 marks). C1: Δθ = 60 K. A1: E = 1.76 × 10⁵ J.', 2, NULL, NULL),
('667085da-09f3-5b42-8b98-2aaabbbac594', '732e83dd-2c22-497f-8d64-2f7a3fd69629', 'ea4a2b5f-fc7b-5e52-84b7-b92896426bdd', 'a-level', '19.6', '9702 coursebook 19.6 · Specific heat capacity and latent heat', 16, 16, 'A 1.2 kW heater warms a 2.0 kg block of oil for 3.0 minutes. Calculate the energy supplied.', NULL, 1, 'numeric'::answer_type, '2.16e5', 'J', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'E = Pt = 1200 × 180 = 216000 J. Mark scheme (2 marks). C1: t in s. A1: E = 2.16 × 10⁵ J.', 2, NULL, NULL),
('d888d683-64c0-5d02-ae3a-bfd98a111423', '732e83dd-2c22-497f-8d64-2f7a3fd69629', 'ea4a2b5f-fc7b-5e52-84b7-b92896426bdd', 'a-level', '19.6', '9702 coursebook 19.6 · Specific heat capacity and latent heat', 17, 17, 'Why does the sea warm up more slowly than the land on a sunny day?', NULL, 1, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'Water has a much higher specific heat capacity than rock and soil', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'The same energy per kilogram produces a smaller temperature rise in water. Mark scheme (1 mark). B1: B (Water has a much higher specific heat capacity than rock and soil).', 1, NULL, NULL),
('4c62143f-db8f-52cb-8a47-78972475c513', '732e83dd-2c22-497f-8d64-2f7a3fd69629', 'ea4a2b5f-fc7b-5e52-84b7-b92896426bdd', 'a-level', '19.6', '9702 coursebook 19.6 · Specific heat capacity and latent heat', 18, 18, 'How much energy is released when 0.30 kg of water cools from 90 °C to 20 °C? Take the specific heat capacity of water as 4200 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹.', NULL, 1, 'numeric'::answer_type, '88200', 'J', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'E = mcΔθ = 0.30 × 4200 × 70 = 88200 J. Mark scheme (2 marks). C1: E = mcΔθ. A1: E = 8.82 × 10⁴ J.', 2, NULL, NULL),
('a2cd03a0-ba4a-5e35-b8e5-ee0e1787c8f8', '732e83dd-2c22-497f-8d64-2f7a3fd69629', 'ea4a2b5f-fc7b-5e52-84b7-b92896426bdd', 'a-level', '19.6', '9702 coursebook 19.6 · Specific heat capacity and latent heat', 19, 19, 'Which quantity is measured in J kg⁻¹?', NULL, 1, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'Specific latent heat', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'L = E/m has no temperature in it: J kg⁻¹. Mark scheme (1 mark). B1: C (Specific latent heat).', 1, NULL, NULL),
('a5bb5849-3a6b-5092-9bb6-bf193629ef12', '732e83dd-2c22-497f-8d64-2f7a3fd69629', 'ea4a2b5f-fc7b-5e52-84b7-b92896426bdd', 'a-level', '19.6', '9702 coursebook 19.6 · Specific heat capacity and latent heat', 20, 20, 'A 3.0 kW kettle runs for 2.0 minutes and heats 1.0 kg of water. Calculate the temperature rise, assuming no losses. Take the specific heat capacity of water as 4200 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹.', NULL, 1, 'numeric'::answer_type, '85.71', 'K', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'E = Pt = 3000 × 120 = 360000 J. Δθ = 360000 ÷ (1.0 × 4200) = 85.7 K. Mark scheme (2 marks). C1: E = Pt. C1: Δθ = E/mc.', 2, NULL, NULL),
('be14faca-3a3b-5979-856d-950ead4e50c4', '732e83dd-2c22-497f-8d64-2f7a3fd69629', 'ea4a2b5f-fc7b-5e52-84b7-b92896426bdd', 'a-level', '19.6', '9702 coursebook 19.6 · Specific heat capacity and latent heat', 21, 21, 'An object''s heat capacity C is related to its mass m and specific heat capacity c by…', NULL, 1, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'C = mc', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Heat capacity is the energy per kelvin for the whole object: C = mc. Mark scheme (1 mark). B1: A (C = mc).', 1, NULL, NULL),
('7bf8cee2-947f-5611-91ca-e126e9d94b61', '732e83dd-2c22-497f-8d64-2f7a3fd69629', 'ea4a2b5f-fc7b-5e52-84b7-b92896426bdd', 'a-level', '19.6', '9702 coursebook 19.6 · Specific heat capacity and latent heat', 22, 22, 'Calculate the heat capacity of a 0.80 kg copper saucepan (c = 385 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹).', NULL, 1, 'numeric'::answer_type, '308', 'J K⁻¹', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'C = mc = 0.80 × 385 = 308 J K⁻¹. Mark scheme (2 marks). C1: C = mc. A1: C = 308 J K⁻¹.', 2, NULL, NULL),
('0c1ce951-528d-50ec-a799-faa9db2c092f', '732e83dd-2c22-497f-8d64-2f7a3fd69629', 'ea4a2b5f-fc7b-5e52-84b7-b92896426bdd', 'a-level', '19.6', '9702 coursebook 19.6 · Specific heat capacity and latent heat', 23, 23, 'A 0.25 kg block of ice at 0 °C is melted by a 500 W heater. Calculate the time taken, assuming no losses. Take the specific latent heat of fusion of ice as 3.34 × 10⁵ J kg⁻¹.', NULL, 1, 'numeric'::answer_type, '167', 's', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'E = mL = 0.25 × 3.34 × 10⁵ = 8.35 × 10⁴ J. t = E/P = 8.35 × 10⁴ ÷ 500 = 167 s. Mark scheme (2 marks). C1: E = mL, t = E/P. A1: t = 167 s.', 2, NULL, NULL),
('8d3508db-a655-5fd5-8246-5acd0f7c7891', '732e83dd-2c22-497f-8d64-2f7a3fd69629', 'ea4a2b5f-fc7b-5e52-84b7-b92896426bdd', 'a-level', '19.6', '9702 coursebook 19.6 · Specific heat capacity and latent heat', 24, 24, 'The specific heat capacity of aluminium is about twice that of iron. Equal masses of each receive the same energy. Which is correct?', NULL, 1, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'The iron''s temperature rises about twice as much', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Δθ = E/(mc): half the c gives twice the Δθ. Mark scheme (1 mark). B1: D (The iron''s temperature rises about twice as much).', 1, NULL, NULL),
('06194222-3d45-5b9c-8295-3f39f6d79c27', '732e83dd-2c22-497f-8d64-2f7a3fd69629', 'ea4a2b5f-fc7b-5e52-84b7-b92896426bdd', 'a-level', '19.6', '9702 coursebook 19.6 · Specific heat capacity and latent heat', 25, 25, 'In an electrical method, a 40 W heater in a 1.0 kg aluminium block raises its temperature by 12.5 K in 300 s. Calculate the specific heat capacity of aluminium.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '960', 'J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'E = Pt = 40 × 300 = 12000 J. c = E/(mΔθ) = 12000 ÷ (1.0 × 12.5) = 960 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: E = Pt. C1: c = E/mΔθ. C1: substitution.', 3, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 450 250" role="img" aria-labelledby="fg39926bee-t fg39926bee-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fg39926bee-t">Electrical method</title><desc id="fg39926bee-d">A 1.0 kg aluminium block, insulated, with a 40 W electric heater and a thermometer in holes in the block; an ammeter and voltmeter measure the heater current and p.d.</desc><g transform="translate(0,0)"><rect x="20" y="60" width="180" height="160" rx="0" style="stroke:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke-dasharray:4 3"/><rect x="30" y="70" width="160" height="140" rx="0" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:var(--fig-fill,rgba(29,78,216,0.10));stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="110" y="236" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">insulation</text><rect x="72" y="40" width="16" height="110" rx="0" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="80" y="28" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">heater</text><rect x="141" y="20" width="8" height="120" rx="4" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><circle cx="145" cy="142" r="7" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="145" y="10" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">thermometer</text><text x="110" y="190" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">1.0 kg aluminium</text></g><g transform="translate(230,13)"><line x1="26" y1="48" x2="42" y2="48" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="110" y1="48" x2="126" y2="48" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="42" y1="48" x2="42" y2="90" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="110" y1="48" x2="110" y2="90" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="42" y1="48" x2="58" y2="48" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="94" y1="48" x2="110" y2="48" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><rect x="58" y="41" width="36" height="14" rx="0" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="76" y="24" font-size="14" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">heater</text><line x1="42" y1="90" x2="64" y2="90" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="88" y1="90" x2="110" y2="90" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><circle cx="76" cy="90" r="12" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="76" y="90.5" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor;font-weight:600">V</text><circle cx="42" cy="48" r="3.2" style="fill:currentColor;stroke:none"/><circle cx="110" cy="48" r="3.2" style="fill:currentColor;stroke:none"/><line x1="126" y1="48" x2="148" y2="48" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="172" y1="48" x2="194" y2="48" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><circle cx="160" cy="48" r="12" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="160" y="48.5" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor;font-weight:600">A</text><line x1="26" y1="184" x2="105" y2="184" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="115" y1="184" x2="194" y2="184" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="105" y1="171" x2="105" y2="197" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="115" y1="177" x2="115" y2="191" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.6;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="110" y="208" font-size="14" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">heater: 40 W</text><line x1="26" y1="48" x2="26" y2="184" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="194" y1="48" x2="194" y2="184" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/></g></svg>', 'A 1.0 kg aluminium block, insulated, with a 40 W electric heater and a thermometer in holes in the block; an ammeter and voltmeter measure the heater current and p.d.'),
('574bebc5-6d0f-504d-9f84-911682cdd7f7', '732e83dd-2c22-497f-8d64-2f7a3fd69629', 'ea4a2b5f-fc7b-5e52-84b7-b92896426bdd', 'a-level', '19.6', '9702 coursebook 19.6 · Specific heat capacity and latent heat', 26, 26, 'A heater runs at 12 V and 4.0 A for 5.0 minutes in a 0.80 kg block, raising its temperature from 18 °C to 36 °C. Calculate the specific heat capacity of the block material.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '1000', 'J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'E = VIt = 12 × 4.0 × 300 = 14400 J. c = 14400 ÷ (0.80 × 18) = 1000 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹. Mark scheme (4 marks). C1: E = VIt. C1: Δθ = 18 K. C1: c = E/mΔθ. A1: c = 1000 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹.', 4, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 450 250" role="img" aria-labelledby="fg23fe07ca-t fg23fe07ca-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fg23fe07ca-t">Electrical method</title><desc id="fg23fe07ca-d">A 0.80 kg block, insulated, with a heater and thermometer; the heater runs at 12 V and 4.0 A for 5.0 minutes.</desc><g transform="translate(0,0)"><rect x="20" y="60" width="180" height="160" rx="0" style="stroke:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke-dasharray:4 3"/><rect x="30" y="70" width="160" height="140" rx="0" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:var(--fig-fill,rgba(29,78,216,0.10));stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="110" y="236" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">insulation</text><rect x="72" y="40" width="16" height="110" rx="0" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="80" y="28" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">heater</text><rect x="141" y="20" width="8" height="120" rx="4" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><circle cx="145" cy="142" r="7" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="145" y="10" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">thermometer</text><text x="110" y="190" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">0.80 kg block</text></g><g transform="translate(230,13)"><line x1="26" y1="48" x2="42" y2="48" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="110" y1="48" x2="126" y2="48" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="42" y1="48" x2="42" y2="90" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="110" y1="48" x2="110" y2="90" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="42" y1="48" x2="58" y2="48" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="94" y1="48" x2="110" y2="48" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><rect x="58" y="41" width="36" height="14" rx="0" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="76" y="24" font-size="14" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">heater</text><line x1="42" y1="90" x2="64" y2="90" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="88" y1="90" x2="110" y2="90" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><circle cx="76" cy="90" r="12" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="76" y="90.5" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor;font-weight:600">V</text><circle cx="42" cy="48" r="3.2" style="fill:currentColor;stroke:none"/><circle cx="110" cy="48" r="3.2" style="fill:currentColor;stroke:none"/><line x1="126" y1="48" x2="148" y2="48" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="172" y1="48" x2="194" y2="48" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><circle cx="160" cy="48" r="12" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="160" y="48.5" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor;font-weight:600">A</text><line x1="26" y1="184" x2="105" y2="184" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="115" y1="184" x2="194" y2="184" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="105" y1="171" x2="105" y2="197" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="115" y1="177" x2="115" y2="191" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.6;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="110" y="208" font-size="14" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">heater: 12 V, 4.0 A</text><line x1="26" y1="48" x2="26" y2="184" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="194" y1="48" x2="194" y2="184" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/></g></svg>', 'A 0.80 kg block, insulated, with a heater and thermometer; the heater runs at 12 V and 4.0 A for 5.0 minutes.'),
('c5b7396e-162e-525f-9702-f232d38283f4', '732e83dd-2c22-497f-8d64-2f7a3fd69629', 'ea4a2b5f-fc7b-5e52-84b7-b92896426bdd', 'a-level', '19.6', '9702 coursebook 19.6 · Specific heat capacity and latent heat', 27, 27, 'In an electrical heating experiment the measured specific heat capacity of a metal block is larger than the accepted value. Why?', NULL, 2, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'Energy is lost to the surroundings, so less energy actually heats the block than the electrical energy counted', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'c = E/(mΔθ): Δθ is smaller than it would be with no losses, so c comes out too large. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: energy lost. C1: Δθ too small. A1: B (Energy is lost to the surroundings, so less energy actually heats the block than the electrical energy counted).', 3, NULL, NULL),
('bbadb6c8-43a9-5a78-823a-a08532c83ff9', '732e83dd-2c22-497f-8d64-2f7a3fd69629', 'ea4a2b5f-fc7b-5e52-84b7-b92896426bdd', 'a-level', '19.6', '9702 coursebook 19.6 · Specific heat capacity and latent heat', 28, 28, 'A solar water heater raises the temperature of 150 kg of water from 15 °C to 55 °C in one day. Calculate the energy absorbed. Take the specific heat capacity of water as 4200 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '2.52e7', 'J', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'E = mcΔθ = 150 × 4200 × 40 = 2.52 × 10⁷ J. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: Δθ = 40 K. C1: E = mcΔθ. A1: E = 2.52 × 10⁷ J.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('b1b4f6e5-2e38-52f2-a7d7-e630184c21cf', '732e83dd-2c22-497f-8d64-2f7a3fd69629', 'ea4a2b5f-fc7b-5e52-84b7-b92896426bdd', 'a-level', '19.6', '9702 coursebook 19.6 · Specific heat capacity and latent heat', 29, 29, 'A 2.4 kW kettle containing 1.7 kg of water at 18 °C takes 4.5 minutes to reach 100 °C. Calculate the efficiency of the kettle. Take the specific heat capacity of water as 4200 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '90.35', '%', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Useful energy = 1.7 × 4200 × 82 = 5.85 × 10⁵ J. Input = 2400 × 270 = 6.48 × 10⁵ J. Efficiency = 5.855 × 10⁵ ÷ 648000 × 100 = 90.4 %. Mark scheme (4 marks). C1: useful energy. C1: input energy. C1: ratio × 100. A1: efficiency = 90.4%.', 4, NULL, NULL),
('ab87c69a-edc7-53b5-87e1-b86b0fac9ef8', '732e83dd-2c22-497f-8d64-2f7a3fd69629', 'ea4a2b5f-fc7b-5e52-84b7-b92896426bdd', 'a-level', '19.6', '9702 coursebook 19.6 · Specific heat capacity and latent heat', 30, 30, 'Why does a temperature–time graph for a heated block often curve and level off rather than rising in a straight line?', NULL, 2, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'As the block gets hotter it loses energy to the surroundings faster', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Heat loss increases with the temperature difference from the surroundings, so the rate of rise falls. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: losses increase with temperature. C1: rate of rise falls. A1: C (As the block gets hotter it loses energy to the surroundings faster).', 3, NULL, NULL),
('b03691ae-02bf-5703-8ea1-340f14a2e69e', '732e83dd-2c22-497f-8d64-2f7a3fd69629', 'ea4a2b5f-fc7b-5e52-84b7-b92896426bdd', 'a-level', '19.6', '9702 coursebook 19.6 · Specific heat capacity and latent heat', 31, 31, 'A 1.2 kg block of iron (c = 450 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹) at 200 °C cools to 25 °C. Calculate the energy released and the mass of ice at 0 °C it could melt. Give the mass of ice. Take the specific latent heat of fusion of ice as 3.34 × 10⁵ J kg⁻¹.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '0.2829', 'kg', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'E = 1.2 × 450 × 175 = 9.45 × 10⁴ J. Mass of ice = E/L = 9.450 × 10⁴ ÷ 3.34 × 10⁵ = 0.283 kg. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: E = mcΔθ. C1: m = E/L. C1: substitution.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('610a50ee-6acc-54db-a93b-ff667708491f', '732e83dd-2c22-497f-8d64-2f7a3fd69629', 'ea4a2b5f-fc7b-5e52-84b7-b92896426bdd', 'a-level', '19.6', '9702 coursebook 19.6 · Specific heat capacity and latent heat', 32, 32, 'A heat pack contains 0.15 kg of a salt solution that solidifies at 54 °C, releasing latent heat of 2.6 × 10⁵ J kg⁻¹, then cools to 30 °C (c of solid = 2200 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹). Calculate the total energy released.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '46920', 'J', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Freezing: 0.15 × 2.6 × 10⁵ = 39000 J. Cooling: 0.15 × 2200 × 24 = 7920 J. Total = 39000 + 7920 = 46920 J. Mark scheme (4 marks). C1: mL. C1: mcΔθ. C1: total. A1: E = 4.69 × 10⁴ J.', 4, NULL, NULL),
('98cfff76-c4a4-56e6-92e5-470863de567e', '732e83dd-2c22-497f-8d64-2f7a3fd69629', 'ea4a2b5f-fc7b-5e52-84b7-b92896426bdd', 'a-level', '19.6', '9702 coursebook 19.6 · Specific heat capacity and latent heat', 33, 33, 'A cup of tea at 80 °C has a few ice cubes added. Which process absorbs the most energy per kilogram of ice?', NULL, 2, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'Melting the ice', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'L_f = 3.34 × 10⁵ J kg⁻¹ is much larger than c × Δθ for small Δθ (2100 × 5 or 4200 × 10). Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: compare mL with mcΔθ. C1: latent heat largest. A1: A (Melting the ice).', 3, NULL, NULL),
('1ae9360b-936a-52be-887b-e24168b9c96a', '732e83dd-2c22-497f-8d64-2f7a3fd69629', 'ea4a2b5f-fc7b-5e52-84b7-b92896426bdd', 'a-level', '19.6', '9702 coursebook 19.6 · Specific heat capacity and latent heat', 34, 34, 'A 70 kg person has an average specific heat capacity of 3500 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹. During exercise their temperature rises by 1.5 K. Calculate the energy stored in the body.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '3.675e5', 'J', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'E = mcΔθ = 70 × 3500 × 1.5 = 367500 J. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: E = mcΔθ. C1: substitution. A1: E = 3.68 × 10⁵ J.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('2e8880a9-e30c-5684-b4d5-acbb1219576d', '732e83dd-2c22-497f-8d64-2f7a3fd69629', 'ea4a2b5f-fc7b-5e52-84b7-b92896426bdd', 'a-level', '19.6', '9702 coursebook 19.6 · Specific heat capacity and latent heat', 35, 35, 'Water flows through a 9.0 kW electric shower at 0.050 kg s⁻¹, entering at 12 °C. Calculate the outlet temperature, assuming no losses. Take the specific heat capacity of water as 4200 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '54.86', '°C', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'P = (m/t)cΔθ, so Δθ = 9000 ÷ (0.050 × 4200) = 42.9 K. Outlet = 12 + 42.9 = 54.9 °C. Mark scheme (4 marks). C1: P = (m/t)cΔθ. C1: Δθ. C1: add to inlet. A1: θ = 54.9 °C.', 4, NULL, NULL),
('34de2444-b06e-56b6-9dc7-519fde4f5836', '732e83dd-2c22-497f-8d64-2f7a3fd69629', 'ea4a2b5f-fc7b-5e52-84b7-b92896426bdd', 'a-level', '19.6', '9702 coursebook 19.6 · Specific heat capacity and latent heat', 36, 36, 'Why does sweating cool the body much more than the same mass of water simply warming up on the skin?', NULL, 2, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'Evaporation takes the latent heat of vaporisation, which is far larger than cΔθ for a few kelvin', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Each kg evaporated removes about 2.4 MJ; warming 1 kg of water by 5 K removes only 21 kJ. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: latent heat. C1: much larger than mcΔθ. A1: D (Evaporation takes the latent heat of vaporisation, which is far larger than cΔθ for a few kelvin).', 3, NULL, NULL),
('c35d31f3-ed26-5e9a-a91c-5bb68b8571a8', '732e83dd-2c22-497f-8d64-2f7a3fd69629', 'ea4a2b5f-fc7b-5e52-84b7-b92896426bdd', 'a-level', '19.6', '9702 coursebook 19.6 · Specific heat capacity and latent heat', 37, 37, 'The temperature of a 0.50 kg block rises by 8.0 K when a 25 W heater runs for 160 s. Calculate the heat capacity of the block, and its specific heat capacity. Give the specific heat capacity.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '1000', 'J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'C = E/Δθ = 25 × 160 ÷ 8.0 = 500 J K⁻¹. c = C/m = 500 ÷ 0.50 = 1000 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: E = Pt. C1: C = E/Δθ. C1: c = C/m.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('62f68027-baaf-52a6-a8e9-5b7e9535f895', '732e83dd-2c22-497f-8d64-2f7a3fd69629', 'ea4a2b5f-fc7b-5e52-84b7-b92896426bdd', 'a-level', '19.6', '9702 coursebook 19.6 · Specific heat capacity and latent heat', 38, 38, 'A 500 g mug of tea at 85 °C is left to cool to 45 °C. Treat it as water. The mug itself (0.30 kg, c = 800 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹) also cools. Calculate the total energy lost to the room. Take the specific heat capacity of water as 4200 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '93600', 'J', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Tea: 0.50 × 4200 × 40 = 84000 J. Mug: 0.30 × 800 × 40 = 9600 J. Total = 84000 + 9600 = 93600 J. Mark scheme (4 marks). C1: tea. C1: mug. C1: total. A1: E = 9.36 × 10⁴ J.', 4, NULL, NULL),
('aa9b2487-b1dc-5575-a4d6-013d469f5776', '732e83dd-2c22-497f-8d64-2f7a3fd69629', 'ea4a2b5f-fc7b-5e52-84b7-b92896426bdd', 'a-level', '19.6', '9702 coursebook 19.6 · Specific heat capacity and latent heat', 39, 39, 'Which two measurements, besides the mass, are needed to find a specific heat capacity by the electrical method?', NULL, 2, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'The electrical energy supplied (V, I, t) and the temperature rise', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'c = E/(mΔθ) and the electrical energy supplied is E = VIt, so c = VIt/(mΔθ): you need the energy supplied (voltage, current and time) and the rise in temperature. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: energy input. C1: temperature rise. A1: B (The electrical energy supplied (V, I, t) and the temperature rise).', 3, NULL, NULL),
('88a3eb9e-f7e8-52c9-b21d-8eacdc48068a', '732e83dd-2c22-497f-8d64-2f7a3fd69629', 'ea4a2b5f-fc7b-5e52-84b7-b92896426bdd', 'a-level', '19.6', '9702 coursebook 19.6 · Specific heat capacity and latent heat', 40, 40, 'A 0.20 kg block of ice at −20 °C is warmed to 0 °C and melted. c_ice = 2100 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹. Calculate the total energy needed. Take the specific latent heat of fusion of ice as 3.34 × 10⁵ J kg⁻¹.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '75200', 'J', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Warming: 0.20 × 2100 × 20 = 8400 J. Melting: 0.20 × 3.34 × 10⁵ = 6.68 × 10⁴ J. Total = 8400 + 6.680 × 10⁴ = 7.52 × 10⁴ J. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: mcΔθ. C1: mL. C1: total.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('63edf82f-fdeb-519e-a2bf-5bf4a5eb0438', '732e83dd-2c22-497f-8d64-2f7a3fd69629', 'ea4a2b5f-fc7b-5e52-84b7-b92896426bdd', 'a-level', '19.6', '9702 coursebook 19.6 · Specific heat capacity and latent heat', 41, 41, 'A student heats 0.50 kg of water in a 0.20 kg aluminium pan (c = 900 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹) on a 1.2 kW hotplate. Both start at 20 °C. (a) Calculate the energy to bring both to 100 °C. (b) Calculate the time taken, assuming 70 % efficiency. (c) The hotplate is left on. Calculate the extra time to boil away 0.10 kg of water. Give only the answer to (c). Take the specific heat capacity of water as 4200 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹. Take the specific latent heat of vaporisation of water as 2.26 × 10⁶ J kg⁻¹.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '269', 's', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) water: 0.50 × 4200 × 80 = 168000 J, pan: 0.20 × 900 × 80 = 14400 J, total = 1.82 × 10⁵ J. (b) useful power = 0.70 × 1200 = 840 W, t = 182400 ÷ 840 = 217 s. (c) E = 0.10 × 2.26 × 10⁶ = 2.26 × 10⁵ J, t = 2.26 × 10⁵ ÷ 840 = 269 s. Mark scheme (6 marks). C1: water and pan. A1: total energy. C1: useful power. A1: t. C1: E = mL_v. A1: t = 269 s.', 6, NULL, NULL),
('40586c31-f84b-5c1e-8a3f-ed94de254b4b', '732e83dd-2c22-497f-8d64-2f7a3fd69629', 'ea4a2b5f-fc7b-5e52-84b7-b92896426bdd', 'a-level', '19.6', '9702 coursebook 19.6 · Specific heat capacity and latent heat', 42, 42, 'A copper block of mass 0.60 kg is heated by a 50 W heater. A graph of temperature against time is a straight line of gradient 0.20 K s⁻¹ for the first few minutes. (a) Calculate c for copper from this gradient. (b) Explain why only the early part of the graph is used. (c) The accepted value is 385 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹. Calculate the percentage difference. Give only the answer to (a).', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '416.7', 'J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) P = mc(dθ/dt), so c = P/(m × gradient) = 50 ÷ (0.60 × 0.20) = 417 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹. (b) At first the block is close to room temperature, so heat losses are smallest. (c) (416.7 − 385) ÷ 385 × 100 = 8.23 %. Mark scheme (5 marks). C1: P = mc dθ/dt. A1: c. B1: small losses early on. C1: percentage difference. A1: c = 417 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹.', 5, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 400 290" role="img" aria-labelledby="fgfe071476-t fgfe071476-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fgfe071476-t">Temperature against time</title><desc id="fgfe071476-d">Graph of temperature against time for the 0.60 kg copper block heated at 50 W: a straight line of gradient 0.20 K s⁻¹ (the starting temperature is drawn at 20 °C).</desc><line x1="29.4" y1="34" x2="29.4" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="109.6" y1="34" x2="109.6" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="189.7" y1="34" x2="189.7" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="269.8" y1="34" x2="269.8" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="349.9" y1="34" x2="349.9" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="29.4" y1="242" x2="382" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="29.4" y1="182.6" x2="382" y2="182.6" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="29.4" y1="123.1" x2="382" y2="123.1" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="29.4" y1="63.7" x2="382" y2="63.7" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="29.4" y1="242" x2="388" y2="242" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:1.4;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="29.4" y1="242" x2="29.4" y2="28" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:1.4;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="392,242 385.7,245 385.7,239" style="fill:currentColor;stroke:none"/><polygon points="29.4,24 32.4,30.3 26.5,30.3" style="fill:currentColor;stroke:none"/><line x1="29.4" y1="242" x2="29.4" y2="246" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="29.4" y="254" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">0</text><line x1="109.6" y1="242" x2="109.6" y2="246" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="109.6" y="254" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">50</text><line x1="189.7" y1="242" x2="189.7" y2="246" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="189.7" y="254" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">100</text><line x1="269.8" y1="242" x2="269.8" y2="246" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="269.8" y="254" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">150</text><line x1="349.9" y1="242" x2="349.9" y2="246" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="349.9" y="254" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">200</text><line x1="25.4" y1="242" x2="29.4" y2="242" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="22.4" y="242" font-size="12" text-anchor="end" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">0</text><line x1="25.4" y1="182.6" x2="29.4" y2="182.6" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="22.4" y="182.6" font-size="12" text-anchor="end" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">20</text><line x1="25.4" y1="123.1" x2="29.4" y2="123.1" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="22.4" y="123.1" font-size="12" text-anchor="end" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">40</text><line x1="25.4" y1="63.7" x2="29.4" y2="63.7" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="22.4" y="63.7" font-size="12" text-anchor="end" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">60</text><text x="205.7" y="274" font-size="14" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">t / s</text><text x="25.4" y="14" font-size="14" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">θ / °C</text><polyline points="29.4,182.6 349.9,63.7" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="201.7" y="131.1" font-size="12" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">gradient 0.20 K s⁻¹</text></svg>', 'Graph of temperature against time for the 0.60 kg copper block heated at 50 W: a straight line of gradient 0.20 K s⁻¹ (the starting temperature is drawn at 20 °C).'),
('f53faa00-610a-5e90-ae4a-7e06560b9245', '732e83dd-2c22-497f-8d64-2f7a3fd69629', 'ea4a2b5f-fc7b-5e52-84b7-b92896426bdd', 'a-level', '19.6', '9702 coursebook 19.6 · Specific heat capacity and latent heat', 43, 43, 'An ice cream maker removes energy from 1.0 kg of mixture (c = 3300 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹, L_f = 2.0 × 10⁵ J kg⁻¹, freezes at −2 °C) starting at 18 °C, freezing all of it and cooling it to −10 °C (c of frozen = 1800 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹). (a) Calculate the energy removed in each stage. (b) Calculate the total. (c) The freezer removes 150 W. Calculate the time taken in minutes. Give only the answer to (c).', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '31.16', 'minutes', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) cool liquid 18 → −2 °C: 1.0 × 3300 × 20 = 66000 J; freeze: 1.0 × 2.0 × 10⁵ = 200000 J; cool solid −2 → −10 °C: 1.0 × 1800 × 8 = 14400 J. (b) total = 66000 + 200000 + 14400 = 280400 J. (c) t = 280400 ÷ 150 = 1870 s. In minutes: 1869 ÷ 60 = 31.2. Mark scheme (6 marks). C1: liquid cooling. C1: freezing. C1: solid cooling. A1: total. C1: t = E/P. A1: t = 31.2 minutes.', 6, NULL, NULL),
('1d43bc87-23b5-5bc7-807b-51e5bb1ba0c2', '732e83dd-2c22-497f-8d64-2f7a3fd69629', 'ea4a2b5f-fc7b-5e52-84b7-b92896426bdd', 'a-level', '19.6', '9702 coursebook 19.6 · Specific heat capacity and latent heat', 44, 44, 'A geothermal power plant takes hot water at 180 °C from underground at 50 kg s⁻¹ and returns it at 80 °C. (a) Calculate the rate at which thermal energy is extracted (c = 4200 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹). (b) The plant is 15 % efficient. Calculate the electrical power output. (c) Explain why the water must be kept under pressure. Give only the answer to (b).', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '3.15e6', 'W', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) P = (m/t)cΔθ = 50 × 4200 × 100 = 2.10 × 10⁷ W. (b) 0.15 × 2.100 × 10⁷ = 3.15 × 10⁶ W. (c) At atmospheric pressure water boils at 100 °C; pressure keeps it liquid at 180 °C. Mark scheme (5 marks). C1: (m/t)cΔθ. A1: thermal power. C1: × efficiency. A1: P = 3.15 × 10⁶ W. B1: boiling point raised by pressure.', 5, NULL, NULL),
('2977936e-d8a1-5f34-a72c-6c83de604067', '732e83dd-2c22-497f-8d64-2f7a3fd69629', 'ea4a2b5f-fc7b-5e52-84b7-b92896426bdd', 'a-level', '19.6', '9702 coursebook 19.6 · Specific heat capacity and latent heat', 45, 45, 'A 0.050 kg lead bullet (c = 130 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹, melting point 327 °C, L_f = 2.5 × 10⁴ J kg⁻¹) at 27 °C hits a steel plate at speed v and stops. Assume all its kinetic energy becomes its internal energy. (a) Calculate the energy to raise it to its melting point. (b) Calculate the energy to melt it completely. (c) Calculate the minimum speed for the bullet to melt completely. Give only the answer to (c).', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '357.8', 'm s⁻¹', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) 0.050 × 130 × 300 = 1950 J. (b) 0.050 × 2.5 × 10⁴ = 1250 J. (c) ½mv² = 1950 + 1250 = 3200 J, v = √(2 × 3200 ÷ 0.050) = 358 m s⁻¹. Mark scheme (6 marks). C1: mcΔθ. A1: 1950 J. A1: 1250 J. M1: ½mv² = total. C1: rearranged. A1: v = 358 m s⁻¹.', 6, NULL, NULL),
('110228c4-2ccc-5a3c-b305-5b365b7cc00c', '732e83dd-2c22-497f-8d64-2f7a3fd69629', 'ea4a2b5f-fc7b-5e52-84b7-b92896426bdd', 'a-level', '19.6', '9702 coursebook 19.6 · Specific heat capacity and latent heat', 46, 46, 'A 1.5 kW electric storage heater contains 40 kg of bricks (c = 840 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹). It charges overnight from 20 °C to 220 °C. (a) Calculate the energy stored. (b) Calculate the charging time with no losses. (c) The bricks release this energy over 10 hours. Calculate the average heating power. Give only the answer to (c).', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '186.7', 'W', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) E = 40 × 840 × 200 = 6.72 × 10⁶ J. (b) t = 6.72 × 10⁶ ÷ 1500 = 4480 s. (c) P = 6.72 × 10⁶ ÷ 36000 = 187 W. Mark scheme (5 marks). C1: E = mcΔθ. A1: E. A1: t. C1: P = E/t. A1: P = 187 W.', 5, NULL, NULL),
('4ca61461-f8e0-54d3-9ef1-74250843a076', '732e83dd-2c22-497f-8d64-2f7a3fd69629', 'ea4a2b5f-fc7b-5e52-84b7-b92896426bdd', 'a-level', '19.6', '9702 coursebook 19.6 · Specific heat capacity and latent heat', 47, 47, 'A steam iron uses 0.025 kg of water per minute, heating it from 20 °C and turning it all into steam at 100 °C. (a) Calculate the energy per minute to heat the water to 100 °C. (b) Calculate the energy per minute to vaporise it. (c) Calculate the minimum power of the iron''s heater. Give only the answer to (c). Take the specific heat capacity of water as 4200 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹. Take the specific latent heat of vaporisation of water as 2.26 × 10⁶ J kg⁻¹.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '1082', 'W', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) 0.025 × 4200 × 80 = 8400 J. (b) 0.025 × 2.26 × 10⁶ = 5.65 × 10⁴ J. (c) P = (8400 + 5.650 × 10⁴) ÷ 60 = 1080 W. Mark scheme (6 marks). C1: mcΔθ. A1: (a). C1: mL. A1: (b). C1: per second. A1: P = 1080 W.', 6, NULL, NULL),
('ab279bbf-c84d-58d3-b6f6-3fbd7596285d', '732e83dd-2c22-497f-8d64-2f7a3fd69629', 'ea4a2b5f-fc7b-5e52-84b7-b92896426bdd', 'a-level', '19.6', '9702 coursebook 19.6 · Specific heat capacity and latent heat', 48, 48, 'Engineers compare two coolants for a computer: water (c = 4200 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹) and oil (c = 1900 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹). The processor produces 120 W and the coolant may warm by at most 8.0 K. (a) Calculate the mass flow rate of water needed. (b) Calculate the flow rate of oil needed. (c) State which is better and why. Give only the answer to (b).', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '0.007895', 'kg s⁻¹', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) ṁ = P/(cΔθ) = 120 ÷ (4200 × 8.0) = 0.00357 kg s⁻¹. (b) ṁ = 120 ÷ (1900 × 8.0) = 0.00789 kg s⁻¹. (c) Water: its higher c means less coolant must flow (but oil is used where water could short circuit). Mark scheme (5 marks). C1: ṁ = P/cΔθ. A1: water. A1: ṁ = 0.00789 kg s⁻¹. B1: water better. B1: higher c.', 5, NULL, NULL),
('68680da7-2f14-54fe-8162-5d9683ed6865', '732e83dd-2c22-497f-8d64-2f7a3fd69629', 'ea4a2b5f-fc7b-5e52-84b7-b92896426bdd', 'a-level', '19.6', '9702 coursebook 19.6 · Specific heat capacity and latent heat', 49, 49, 'A 2.0 kg block of copper (c = 385 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹) at 150 °C is placed on a large block of ice at 0 °C. (a) Calculate the energy the copper gives out cooling to 0 °C. (b) Calculate the mass of ice melted. (c) A 2.0 kg aluminium block (c = 900 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹) at 150 °C is used instead. Calculate how many times more ice melts. Give only the answer to (b). Take the specific latent heat of fusion of ice as 3.34 × 10⁵ J kg⁻¹.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '0.3458', 'kg', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) E = 2.0 × 385 × 150 = 1.16 × 10⁵ J. (b) m = E/L = 1.155 × 10⁵ ÷ 3.34 × 10⁵ = 0.346 kg. (c) Ratio = 900 ÷ 385 = 2.34 times more ice. Mark scheme (6 marks). C1: E = mcΔθ. A1: E. C1: m = E/L. A1: m. B1: ratio of c. A1: m = 0.346 kg.', 6, NULL, NULL),
('17829e20-2045-59fb-9367-f4c01244e9cf', '732e83dd-2c22-497f-8d64-2f7a3fd69629', 'ea4a2b5f-fc7b-5e52-84b7-b92896426bdd', 'a-level', '19.6', '9702 coursebook 19.6 · Specific heat capacity and latent heat', 50, 50, 'Tea is made by pouring 0.25 kg of water at 100 °C onto tea leaves in a pot. Milk (0.030 kg at 5 °C, c = 3900 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹) is then added. (a) Calculate the temperature of the tea after the milk is added, ignoring other losses. (b) Calculate the energy given to the milk. (c) Explain whether adding the milk earlier or later keeps the tea warmer after 10 minutes. Give only the answer to (a). Take the specific heat capacity of water as 4200 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '90.48', '°C', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) θ = (0.25 × 4200 × 100 + 0.030 × 3900 × 5) ÷ (0.25 × 4200 + 0.030 × 3900) = 90.5 °C. (b) 0.030 × 3900 × (90.48 − 5) = 1.00 × 10⁴ J. (c) Adding milk early lowers the temperature, so the tea then loses heat more slowly; adding it early usually leaves the tea slightly warmer after 10 minutes. Mark scheme (5 marks). M1: heat balance. A1: θ. C1: energy to milk. A1: θ = 90.5 °C. B1: cooling rate depends on temperature difference.', 5, NULL, NULL),
('cb289a0f-7be4-583d-9f59-bfa0f511921a', '732e83dd-2c22-497f-8d64-2f7a3fd69629', 'ea4a2b5f-fc7b-5e52-84b7-b92896426bdd', 'a-level', '19.6', '9702 coursebook 19.6 · Specific heat capacity and latent heat', 51, 51, 'An inventor claims a 70 kg person could boil 1.0 kg of water at 20 °C by running up stairs. (a) Calculate the energy needed to bring the water to the boil (100 °C). (b) Calculate the height the person would need to climb to gain that much gravitational potential energy. (c) Comment on the claim. Give only the answer to (b). Take the specific heat capacity of water as 4200 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹. Take g = 9.81 m s⁻².', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '489.3', 'm', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) E = 1.0 × 4200 × 80 = 336000 J. (b) h = E/(mg) = 336000 ÷ (70 × 9.81) = 489 m. (c) That is a climb of about 490 m — possible, but the energy would have to be converted to heat with 100 % efficiency, which it cannot be; the body''s own efficiency is about 25 %. Mark scheme (6 marks). C1: E = mcΔθ. A1: E. C1: mgh = E. A1: h = 489 m. B1: efficiency comment. B1: realistic conclusion.', 6, NULL, NULL),
('f8be56fa-9e09-53de-8fd5-b9e431f78792', '732e83dd-2c22-497f-8d64-2f7a3fd69629', 'ea4a2b5f-fc7b-5e52-84b7-b92896426bdd', 'a-level', '19.6', '9702 coursebook 19.6 · Specific heat capacity and latent heat', 52, 52, 'A student uses an electrical method on a 1.0 kg aluminium block and plots temperature against time. The straight part has gradient 0.045 K s⁻¹ with a 42 W heater. (a) Calculate c. (b) The accepted value is 900 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹. Calculate the percentage error. (c) Suggest one reason for the error and how to reduce it. Give only the answer to (a).', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '933.3', 'J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) c = P/(m × gradient) = 42 ÷ (1.0 × 0.045) = 933 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹. (b) (933.3 − 900) ÷ 900 × 100 = 3.70 %. (c) Heat losses (or poor thermal contact): insulate the block and use oil in the thermometer hole. Mark scheme (5 marks). C1: P = mc dθ/dt. A1: c. A1: c = 933 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹. B1: reason. B1: improvement.', 5, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 400 290" role="img" aria-labelledby="fg17ae2f7e-t fg17ae2f7e-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fg17ae2f7e-t">Temperature against time</title><desc id="fg17ae2f7e-d">Graph of temperature against time for the 1.0 kg aluminium block heated at 42 W: the straight part has a gradient of 0.045 K s⁻¹ (the starting temperature is drawn at 20 °C).</desc><line x1="29.4" y1="34" x2="29.4" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="83.7" y1="34" x2="83.7" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="137.9" y1="34" x2="137.9" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="192.2" y1="34" x2="192.2" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1