end" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">0</text><line x1="25.4" y1="204.2" x2="29.4" y2="204.2" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="22.4" y="204.2" font-size="12" text-anchor="end" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">10</text><line x1="25.4" y1="166.4" x2="29.4" y2="166.4" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="22.4" y="166.4" font-size="12" text-anchor="end" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">20</text><line x1="25.4" y1="128.5" x2="29.4" y2="128.5" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="22.4" y="128.5" font-size="12" text-anchor="end" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">30</text><line x1="25.4" y1="90.7" x2="29.4" y2="90.7" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="22.4" y="90.7" font-size="12" text-anchor="end" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">40</text><line x1="25.4" y1="52.9" x2="29.4" y2="52.9" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="22.4" y="52.9" font-size="12" text-anchor="end" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">50</text><text x="205.7" y="274" font-size="14" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">t / s</text><text x="25.4" y="14" font-size="14" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">θ / °C</text><polyline points="29.4,166.4 354.9,64.3" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="204.2" y="123.3" font-size="12" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">gradient 0.045 K s⁻¹</text></svg>', 'Graph of temperature against time for the 1.0 kg aluminium block heated at 42 W: the straight part has a gradient of 0.045 K s⁻¹ (the starting temperature is drawn at 20 °C).'),
('ea2705cb-ff3a-5ef0-9dfe-69097d00968d', '732e83dd-2c22-497f-8d64-2f7a3fd69629', 'ea4a2b5f-fc7b-5e52-84b7-b92896426bdd', 'a-level', '19.6', '9702 coursebook 19.6 · Specific heat capacity and latent heat', 53, 53, 'A 0.050 kg ice cube at −10 °C is dropped into 0.30 kg of orange juice (treat as water) at 20 °C. c_ice = 2100 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹. (a) Show that all the ice melts. (b) Calculate the final temperature. (c) Explain why ice at −10 °C cools a drink slightly more than the same mass of ice at 0 °C. Give only the answer to (b). Take the specific heat capacity of water as 4200 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹. Take the specific latent heat of fusion of ice as 3.34 × 10⁵ J kg⁻¹.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '5.068', '°C', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) Juice cooling to 0 °C can give 0.30 × 4200 × 20 = 25200 J. The ice needs 0.050 × 2100 × 10 + 0.050 × 3.34 × 10⁵ = 1050 + 16700 = 17750 J, which is less, so all the ice melts. (b) 0.30 × 4200 × (20 − θ) = 17750 + 0.050 × 4200 × θ: θ = (25200 − 17750) ÷ 1470 = 5.07 °C. (c) It also absorbs energy warming from −10 °C to 0 °C before melting. Mark scheme (6 marks). C1: energy available. C1: energy needed. A1: all melts. M1: energy balance. A1: θ = 5.07 °C. B1: extra energy to warm the ice.', 6, NULL, NULL)
